---
name: native-import-in-fence
description: A module import link written INSIDE a code fence must load the module on every lane
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# Import link inside a code fence

**The `known-red: js` declaration was deleted 2026-08-16, on this suite's own instruction** — it
printed `STALE known-red: this lane now PASSES`, which is the freeze doing what it is for. Measured
on a clean build: int, js, jvm and v2 all pass.

**It does NOT mean `js-jvm-codegen-in-fence-imports-not-followed` is fixed, and the distinction is
the command.** This suite's js lane is `--emit-js` (`run.sc:624`), and `f770dad20` measured that
`emit-js | node` works while `run-js` still dies on the same file — same launcher, same build. So
the entry stays open for the half this case cannot see, and a reader who takes this green as the
whole answer will look in the wrong place.

`BUGS.md` `v2-native-front-in-fence-imports-not-followed`. The Markdown link form
`[names](path.ssc)` is an import wherever it stands — the same statement above the
fence and inside it must load the same module.

The native lane's import scanner used to flip into "skip to the closing fence" mode
on the opening ` ``` `, so an in-fence link was never collected and every imported
name stayed unbound. `v1` resolved it fine, which made this a pure lane divergence:
`bin/ssc run` printed `ssc: unbound global: square` for this exact program while
`ssc-tools run --v1` printed the numbers below.

```scalascript
[square, cube, factorial](./lib/math-utils.ssc)
println(square(5))
println(cube(3))
println(factorial(6))
```

The second fence checks that a *later* fence still sees the module imported by the
first — the scan is over the whole document, not per fence.

```scalascript
println(square(7))
```
